good afternoon and thank you so much for  joining us on this monday at 4 o'clock  i'm kirsten joyce good afternoon i'm  brian loftis the number of nevadans  diagnosed with covid does continue to  climb  we begin with a look at those numbers  here's the latest  today's report showing our state with  more than  sixteen hundred new cases and eight  deaths we've seen that number sit  at over one thousand for almost thirty  days now since march there have been  more than a hundred fifty two thousand  cases  and twenty one hundred deaths now here  in the county we claim about 1100 of  those new daily cases  four new deaths were also reported today  the recovery rate  there at the bottom of your tv sits at  84.8 percent  with those rising numbers hospitals are  getting closer to full and it will take  weeks to learn  the impact that thanksgiving weekend and  all that travel  will have on our hospitals the i-team's  vanessa murphy monitoring the numbers  she joins us live from her home studio  to tell us what health officials are  saying  about the uptick in cases  yeah brian we are seeing record-breaking  numbers here remember that spike in july  and hospitals were strained  the numbers are worse now and they  continue  to climb test positivity rate is over 17  this is the highest since the start of  the pandemic  it's considered too high at even 5  hospitalizations are at a record high in  a call with the state's covet 19  response director caleb cage  he points to a 275 percent  increase in hospitalizations over the  last 30 days in southern nevada and a  surge  is expected according to the federal and  state health officials  with current growth rates and case rate  and several factors such as colder  weather leading to more indoor  activities  and back-to-back holidays numbers are  likely to surge  in the coming weeks  we will not know how thanksgiving day  and the long weekend helped spread the  virus even more because  there is that possible 14-day incubation  period  meaning if someone gets tested today  after being exposed to the virus on  thanksgiving and the test comes back  negative they can still test positive  up to 14 days after now the stay at home  pause issued by governor steve  sissellach began  last tuesday it's three weeks long the  governor has said he wants to avoid  another shutdown  especially in a state like nevada  because we rely so heavily on tourism  but if the numbers continue to go up we  could see  additional restrictions reporting live  vanessa murphy 8 news now
 

